      "title": "Easy XOR Problem",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": false,
      "shortest": false,
      "solution": "#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nlong long power(long long base, long long exp) {\n    long long res = 1;\n    while (exp > 0) {\n        if (exp % 2 == 1) res = (res * base);\n        base = (base * base);\n        exp /= 2;\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n; cin >> n;\n    string s; cin >> s;\n\n    if (s[0] == '0') {\n        cout << 0 << \"\\n\";\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    int target = n - 1;\n    long long ans = 0;\n    int submask = target;\n\n    while (1) {\n        ans = (ans + power(2, submask));\n        if (submask == 0) break;\n        submask = (submask - 1) & target;\n    }\n\n    cout << ans << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",
      "statement": "You are given a binary string [[S]] made of {{0}}s and {{1}}s.\n\nGenerate every possible subsequence of [[S]] by picking any subset of characters while keeping their relative order. \n\nTreat each subsequence as a binary number and convert it to an integer (an empty subsequence counts as {{0}}).\n\nCompute the total bitwise XOR sum of all these integer values.\n\nReference on what bitwise XOR is: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitwise_operation\n\n<<Example:>>\n[[S]] = \"{{101}}\"\nSubsequences: \"\", \"{{1}}\", \"{{0}}\", \"{{1}}\", \"{{10}}\", \"{{11}}\", \"{{01}}\", \"{{101}}\"\nInteger values: {{0}}, {{1}}, {{0}}, {{1}}, {{2}}, {{3}}, {{1}}, {{5}}\nBitwise XOR total: {{0}} XOR {{1}} XOR {{0}} XOR {{1}} XOR {{2}} XOR {{3}} XOR {{1}} XOR {{5}} = {{5}}",
